There are a few ways to add a Toolbar in a Fragment, depending on your app's structure and what you want to achieve:

**Scenario 1: Fragment Has Its Own Independent Toolbar**
This is useful if a specific fragment needs a toolbar that's visually distinct or has functionality not shared with other parts of the app, or if the Activity doesn't have a global Toolbar.

1.  **Add Toolbar to Fragment's Layout (e.g., `fragment_my.xml`)**:
    ```xml
    <LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
        xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:orientation="vertical">

        <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
            android:id="@+id/fragment_toolbar"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
            android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
            app:title="Fragment Toolbar"
            app:titleTextColor="@android:color/white"
            android:theme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Dark.ActionBar"
            app:popupTheme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Light" />

        <!-- Rest of your fragment's content -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="Content of the fragment"
            android:layout_gravity="center"
            android:padding="16dp"/>

    </LinearLayout>
    ```

2.  **Set up the Toolbar in your Fragment's code**:
    Make sure your Activity is using a `NoActionBar` theme (e.g., `Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.NoActionBar`) in `styles.xml` or `AndroidManifest.xml` if you want this fragment's toolbar to be the *only* one visible when this fragment is active.

    ```kotlin
    // MyFragment.kt
    import android.os.Bundle
    import android.view.*
    import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
    import androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar // Important: use androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
    import androidx.fragment.app.Fragment
    import com.yourpackage.R // Replace with your R file

    class MyFragment : Fragment() {

        private lateinit var toolbar: Toolbar

        override fun onCreateView(
            inflater: LayoutInflater, container: ViewGroup?,
            savedInstanceState: Bundle?
        ): View? {
            // Inflate the layout for this fragment
            val view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_my, container, false)
            toolbar = view.findViewById(R.id.fragment_toolbar)
            return view
        }

        override fun onViewCreated(view: View, savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
            super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState)

            // Set this toolbar as the support action bar
            (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.setSupportActionBar(toolbar)
            // Or, if you don't want it to be the main action bar but just a toolbar:
            // toolbar.title = "My Fragment Title"
            // toolbar.setNavigationIcon(R.drawable.ic_arrow_back)
            // toolbar.setNavigationOnClickListener { requireActivity().onBackPressedDispatcher.onBackPressed() }


            // To handle menu items specifically for this fragment's toolbar
            setHasOptionsMenu(true) // Inform the fragment manager that this fragment wants to add items to the options menu

            // Customize the toolbar
            toolbar.title = "Fragment Specific Title"
            // (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.supportActionBar?.setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true) // If you set it as support action bar
            // (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.supportActionBar?.setDisplayShowHomeEnabled(true)

            toolbar.setNavigationOnClickListener {
                // Handle navigation icon press (e.g., back)
                requireActivity().onBackPressedDispatcher.onBackPressed()
            }
        }

        // Inflate menu for this fragment's toolbar
        override fun onCreateOptionsMenu(menu: Menu, inflater: MenuInflater) {
            inflater.inflate(R.menu.fragment_menu, menu) // Create fragment_menu.xml in res/menu
            super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater)
        }

        // Handle menu item clicks for this fragment's toolbar
        override fun onOptionsItemSelected(item: MenuItem): Boolean {
            return when (item.itemId) {
                R.id.action_fragment_settings -> {
                    // Handle settings click
                    true
                }
                // Handle other menu items
                else -> super.onOptionsItemSelected(item)
            }
        }

        override fun onDestroyView() {
            super.onDestroyView()
            // Important: If the Activity has its own toolbar,
            // and you set this fragment's toolbar as the support action bar,
            // you might want to revert to the Activity's toolbar here or clear it.
            // (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.setSupportActionBar(null) // This can be tricky
        }
    }
    ```

    **res/menu/fragment_menu.xml**:
    ```xml
    <menu xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
        xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto">
        <item
            android:id="@+id/action_fragment_settings"
            android:title="Settings"
            app:showAsAction="ifRoom"
            android:icon="@android:drawable/ic_menu_manage" />
    </menu>
    ```

**Scenario 2: Fragment Modifies the Activity's Toolbar**
This is the most common scenario. The Activity owns the Toolbar, and the Fragment customizes its title, menu items, and navigation icon when it becomes visible.

1.  **Activity Layout (e.g., `activity_main.xml`)**:
    ```xml
    <LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
        xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:orientation="vertical">

        <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
            android:id="@+id/activity_toolbar"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
            android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
            android:theme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Dark.ActionBar"
            app:popupTheme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Light" />

        <FrameLayout
            android:id="@+id/fragment_container"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="match_parent" />

    </LinearLayout>
    ```

2.  **Activity Code (e.g., `MainActivity.kt`)**:
    Make sure your Activity uses a `NoActionBar` theme.
    ```kotlin
    // MainActivity.kt
    import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
    import android.os.Bundle
    import androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
    import com.yourpackage.R // Replace with your R file

    class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {
        override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
            super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
            setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)

            val toolbar: Toolbar = findViewById(R.id.activity_toolbar)
            setSupportActionBar(toolbar) // Sets the Toolbar as the ActionBar

            if (savedInstanceState == null) {
                supportFragmentManager.beginTransaction()
                    .replace(R.id.fragment_container, MyFragment())
                    .commit()
            }
        }

        // This is needed if you want the fragment to handle options menu items
        // The activity will delegate to the fragment
        /*
        override fun onCreateOptionsMenu(menu: Menu?): Boolean {
             // Activity can inflate its own global menu items here if needed
             // menuInflater.inflate(R.menu.activity_menu, menu)
             return super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu)
        }

        override fun onOptionsItemSelected(item: MenuItem): Boolean {
            // Handle activity global menu items here
            // Or, if using Jetpack Navigation, this can be handled by NavigationUI
            return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item)
        }
        */
    }
    ```

3.  **Fragment Code (e.g., `MyFragment.kt`)**:
    ```kotlin
    // MyFragment.kt
    import android.os.Bundle
    import android.view.*
    import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
    import androidx.fragment.app.Fragment
    import com.yourpackage.R // Replace with your R file

    class MyFragment : Fragment() {

        override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
            super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
            setHasOptionsMenu(true) // IMPORTANT: To make fragment's onCreateOptionsMenu called
        }

        override fun onCreateView(
            inflater: LayoutInflater, container: ViewGroup?,
            savedInstanceState: Bundle?
        ): View? {
            // Inflate a layout that DOES NOT contain its own Toolbar
            return inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_content_only, container, false)
        }

        override fun onViewCreated(view: View, savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
            super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState)

            // Access the Activity's ActionBar
            val actionBar = (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.supportActionBar
            actionBar?.title = "Fragment Title"
            actionBar?.setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true) // Show back button
            actionBar?.setDisplayShowHomeEnabled(true)
            // You can also set a custom navigation icon:
            // actionBar?.setHomeAsUpIndicator(R.drawable.ic_custom_back)
        }

        override fun onCreateOptionsMenu(menu: Menu, inflater: MenuInflater) {
            inflater.inflate(R.menu.fragment_menu, menu) // Your fragment-specific menu
            super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater)
        }

        override fun onOptionsItemSelected(item: MenuItem): Boolean {
            return when (item.itemId) {
                android.R.id.home -> { // Handle Up/Back button press on the toolbar
                    requireActivity().onBackPressedDispatcher.onBackPressed()
                    true
                }
                R.id.action_fragment_settings -> {
                    // Handle settings
                    true
                }
                else -> super.onOptionsItemSelected(item)
            }
        }

        override fun onResume() {
            super.onResume()
            // Optionally, re-apply toolbar settings if they might be changed by other fragments
            val actionBar = (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.supportActionBar
            actionBar?.title = "Fragment Title" // Ensure title is set
            // Invalidate options menu if it needs to be redrawn due to fragment changes
            // requireActivity().invalidateOptionsMenu() // Use if menu content changes dynamically
        }

        override fun onPause() {
            super.onPause()
            // Optional: Clear fragment-specific toolbar items or reset title
            // (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.supportActionBar?.title = "App Name"
            // (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.supportActionBar?.setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(false)
        }
    }
    ```
    **`fragment_content_only.xml`**:
    ```xml
    <LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:gravity="center"
        android:orientation="vertical">

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="Content of the fragment (no toolbar here)" />

    </LinearLayout>
    ```

**Scenario 3: Using Jetpack Navigation Component**
This is the recommended modern approach as it handles much of the Toolbar setup (like titles and Up button) automatically.

1.  **Activity Layout (e.g., `activity_main.xml`)**:
    ```xml
    <LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
        xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:orientation="vertical">

        <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
            android:id="@+id/toolbar"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
            android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
            android:theme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Dark.ActionBar"
            app:popupTheme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Light" />

        <androidx.fragment.app.FragmentContainerView
            android:id="@+id/nav_host_fragment"
            android:name="androidx.navigation.fragment.NavHostFragment"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="match_parent"
            app:defaultNavHost="true"
            app:navGraph="@navigation/nav_graph" />
    </LinearLayout>
    ```

2.  **Navigation Graph (e.g., `res/navigation/nav_graph.xml`)**:
    Set the `android:label` for each fragment destination. This label will be used as the Toolbar title.
    ```xml
    <navigation xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
        xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
        xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
        android:id="@+id/nav_graph"
        app:startDestination="@id/firstFragment">

        <fragment
            android:id="@+id/firstFragment"
            android:name="com.example.FirstFragment"
            android:label="First Fragment Title"
            tools:layout="@layout/fragment_first" />

        <fragment
            android:id="@+id/secondFragment"
            android:name="com.example.SecondFragment"
            android:label="Second Fragment"
            tools:layout="@layout/fragment_second" />
    </navigation>
    ```

3.  **Activity Code (e.g., `MainActivity.kt`)**:
    ```kotlin
    // MainActivity.kt
    import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
    import android.os.Bundle
    import androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
    import androidx.navigation.NavController
    import androidx.navigation.fragment.NavHostFragment
    import androidx.navigation.ui.AppBarConfiguration
    import androidx.navigation.ui.NavigationUI
    import androidx.navigation.ui.setupActionBarWithNavController
    import com.yourpackage.R // Replace

    class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

        private lateinit var navController: NavController
        private lateinit var appBarConfiguration: AppBarConfiguration

        override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
            super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
            setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)

            val toolbar: Toolbar = findViewById(R.id.toolbar)
            setSupportActionBar(toolbar)

            val navHostFragment = supportFragmentManager
                .findFragmentById(R.id.nav_host_fragment) as NavHostFragment
            navController = navHostFragment.navController

            // Define top-level destinations (usually those in BottomNavigationView or Drawer)
            // The Up button will not be shown for these destinations.
            appBarConfiguration = AppBarConfiguration(
                setOf(R.id.firstFragment /*, R.id.anotherTopLevelFragment */) // Add your top-level fragment IDs
                // or pass navController.graph directly if you want Up button on all non-start destinations
                // appBarConfiguration = AppBarConfiguration(navController.graph)
            )

            setupActionBarWithNavController(navController, appBarConfiguration)
        }

        override fun onSupportNavigateUp(): Boolean {
            // Handles Up button navigation
            return NavigationUI.navigateUp(navController, appBarConfiguration) || super.onSupportNavigateUp()
        }
    }
    ```

4.  **Fragment (Optional Menu Items)**:
    Fragments can still add their own menu items.
    ```kotlin
    // FirstFragment.kt
    import android.os.Bundle
    import android.view.*
    import androidx.fragment.app.Fragment
    import com.yourpackage.R

    class FirstFragment : Fragment() {
        override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
            super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
            setHasOptionsMenu(true) // Enable menu for this fragment
        }

        override fun onCreateView(inflater: LayoutInflater, container: ViewGroup?, savedInstanceState: Bundle?): View? {
            return inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_first, container, false)
        }

        override fun onCreateOptionsMenu(menu: Menu, inflater: MenuInflater) {
            inflater.inflate(R.menu.fragment_menu, menu) // Your fragment-specific menu
            super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater)
        }

        override fun onOptionsItemSelected(item: MenuItem): Boolean {
            return when (item.itemId) {
                R.id.action_fragment_settings -> {
                    // Handle settings
                    true
                }
                else -> super.onOptionsItemSelected(item)
            }
        }
    }
    ```

**Key Considerations**:
*   **Theme**: If your Activity provides the Toolbar, ensure it uses a `Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar` or `Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.NoActionBar` (or similar) theme in `styles.xml` or `AndroidManifest.xml` for the Activity. Then, you define the Toolbar in your Activity's layout.
*   `setHasOptionsMenu(true)`: Call this in your Fragment's `onCreate()` or `onViewCreated()` if the fragment needs to contribute items to the options menu (whether it's the Activity's toolbar or its own).
*   **Material Components**: Prefer `com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar` over `androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar` for better Material Design integration.
*   **Lifecycle**: Be mindful of the Fragment lifecycle. Setup Toolbar elements in `onViewCreated()` or `onResume()`, and potentially clean them up in `onPause()` or `onDestroyView()` if they are fragment-specific and could interfere with other fragments using the same Activity toolbar.

Choose the scenario that best fits your application's navigation and UI structure. For most modern apps, **Scenario 3 (Jetpack Navigation Component)** is highly recommended for its simplicity and integration with navigation patterns.

Okay, here's a JavaScript function that finds an object by its key name deep within a given object without using recursion. It uses an iterative approach (specifically, a Depth-First Search strategy using a stack) and returns both the found object and the path to it.

```javascript
function findObjectByNameIterative(rootObject, targetName) {
  if (!rootObject || typeof rootObject !== 'object' || rootObject === null) {
    return { foundObject: null, path: null }; // Or throw an error
  }

  // Stack will hold objects to visit, along with their paths
  // Each item in the stack will be: { node: objectToInspect, currentPath: [...] }
  const stack = [{ node: rootObject, currentPath: [] }];

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const { node, currentPath } = stack.pop(); // Get the last added item (DFS)

    // Check if the current node is not null and is an object
    if (node && typeof node === 'object') {
      // Iterate over the keys of the current node
      for (const key in node) {
        // Ensure it's an own property, not from prototype chain
        if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(node, key)) {
          const value = node[key];
          const newPath = [...currentPath, key]; // Path to the current value

          // Check if the *key* is the targetName and its *value* is an object
          if (key === targetName && typeof value === 'object' && value !== null) {
            return { foundObject: value, path: newPath };
          }

          // If the value is an object (or array), add it to the stack to explore later
          // This includes arrays, as they are objects and might contain objects with the target key
          if (typeof value === 'object' && value !== null) {
            stack.push({ node: value, currentPath: newPath });
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // If the loop finishes, the target was not found
  return { foundObject: null, path: null };
}

// --- Example Usage ---

const complexObject = {
  id: 'root',
  data: {
    user: {
      id: 1,
      profile: {
        name: 'Alice',
        settings: {
          theme: 'dark',
          notifications: {
            email: true,
            sms: false,
            // Target object we are looking for by key "preferences"
            preferences: { 
              layout: 'compact',
              language: 'en'
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    items: [
      { id: 'item1', name: 'Laptop' },
      { 
        id: 'item2', 
        name: 'Mouse',
        // Another target object, deeper inside an array
        details: {
            preferences: { 
                color: 'black',
                dpi: 1600
            }
        }
      }
    ],
    config: null, // To test null values
    anotherLevel: {
        yetAnother: {
            // And another one
            preferences: {
                sound: 'off'
            }
        }
    }
  }
};

console.log("--- Searching for 'preferences' ---");
const result1 = findObjectByNameIterative(complexObject, 'preferences');
if (result1.foundObject) {
  console.log("Found 'preferences':", result1.foundObject);
  console.log("Path:", result1.path.join('.')); // e.g., data.user.profile.settings.notifications.preferences
} else {
  console.log("'preferences' not found.");
}

console.log("\n--- Searching for 'user' ---");
const result2 = findObjectByNameIterative(complexObject, 'user');
if (result2.foundObject) {
  console.log("Found 'user':", result2.foundObject);
  console.log("Path:", result2.path.join('.')); // e.g., data.user
} else {
  console.log("'user' not found.");
}

console.log("\n--- Searching for 'nonExistentKey' ---");
const result3 = findObjectByNameIterative(complexObject, 'nonExistentKey');
if (result3.foundObject) {
  console.log("Found 'nonExistentKey':", result3.foundObject);
  console.log("Path:", result3.path.join('.'));
} else {
  console.log("'nonExistentKey' not found.");
}

console.log("\n--- Searching in a simple object ---");
const simpleObj = {
    myTarget: { value: 123 }
};
const result4 = findObjectByNameIterative(simpleObj, 'myTarget');
if (result4.foundObject) {
  console.log("Found 'myTarget':", result4.foundObject);
  console.log("Path:", result4.path.join('.')); // e.g., myTarget
} else {
  console.log("'myTarget' not found.");
}

console.log("\n--- Searching in an empty object ---");
const result5 = findObjectByNameIterative({}, 'myTarget');
console.log(result5); // { foundObject: null, path: null }

console.log("\n--- Searching with null input ---");
const result6 = findObjectByNameIterative(null, 'myTarget');
console.log(result6); // { foundObject: null, path: null }

console.log("\n--- Searching for a key whose value is not an object ---");
const result7 = findObjectByNameIterative(complexObject, 'name'); // 'name' exists, but its value is a string
console.log(result7); // { foundObject: null, path: null } because we only return if value is an object
```

**How it Works (Iterative DFS):**

1.  **Stack Initialization:**
    *   We create a `stack`. Each element in the stack will be an object `{ node, currentPath }`.
    *   `node`: The object (or array) currently being inspected.
    *   `currentPath`: An array of keys representing the path taken from the `rootObject` to reach this `node`.
    *   We initialize the stack with the `rootObject` and an empty path: `[{ node: rootObject, currentPath: [] }]`.

2.  **Main Loop (`while (stack.length > 0)`):**
    *   The loop continues as long as there are items in the stack to process.
    *   **`stack.pop()`:** We take the *last* item added to the stack. This makes it a Depth-First Search (DFS) because we explore one branch as deeply as possible before backtracking.
    *   We destructure `node` and `currentPath` from the popped item.

3.  **Inspecting the Current Node:**
    *   We check if `node` is actually an object (and not `null`).
    *   **`for...in` loop:** We iterate over all enumerable properties (keys) of the `node`.
    *   **`Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(node, key)`:** This is a crucial check to ensure we are only processing properties directly defined on the object, not inherited ones.
    *   **`value = node[key]`:** Get the value associated with the current key.
    *   **`newPath = [...currentPath, key]`:** We construct the path to this `value` by appending the current `key` to the `currentPath`.

4.  **Checking for the Target:**
    *   **`if (key === targetName && typeof value === 'object' && value !== null)`:**
        *   This is the core condition. We check if the current `key` matches the `targetName` we're looking for.
        *   AND, importantly, we check if the `value` associated with that `key` is itself an object (and not `null`). The problem asks to find an *object* by its name.
        *   If both conditions are true, we've found our target! We return an object containing `foundObject: value` and `path: newPath`.

5.  **Adding Children to the Stack:**
    *   **`if (typeof value === 'object' && value !== null)`:**
        *   If the `value` of the current property is an object (this includes arrays, since `typeof []` is `'object'`), it means this branch can be explored further.
        *   We push a new item onto the stack: `{ node: value, currentPath: newPath }`. This schedules the `value` (which is a nested object or array) to be inspected later, along with the path taken to reach it.

6.  **Not Found:**
    *   If the `while` loop finishes, it means the stack became empty, and we didn't find the `targetName` associated with an object value. In this case, we return `{ foundObject: null, path: null }`.

This iterative approach effectively simulates recursion using an explicit stack, avoiding potential stack overflow errors for very deep objects and fulfilling the "no recursion" requirement.

instruction:
If I have a TypeScript class:

class Foo {
  ReactProperties: {
    a: string;
  }
}

How do I extract the type of the ReactProperties member object from the type Class?

output:
You can use an **indexed access type** (also sometimes called a lookup type) to extract the type of a property from another type.

For your `Foo` class, you would do it like this:

```typescript
class Foo {
  ReactProperties: {
    a: string;
  }
  // To make this class instantiable, let's add a constructor or initializer
  constructor() {
    this.ReactProperties = { a: "default" };
  }
}

// Extract the type of ReactProperties
type FooReactPropertiesType = Foo['ReactProperties'];

// Now FooReactPropertiesType is equivalent to:
// {
//   a: string;
// }

// You can use this type:
const myProps: FooReactPropertiesType = {
  a: "hello"
};

// const wrongProps: FooReactPropertiesType = { // Error: Property 'a' is missing
//   b: "world"
// };

// const anotherWrongProps: FooReactPropertiesType = { // Error: Type 'number' is not assignable to type 'string'.
//  a: 123
// };

console.log(myProps.a); // Output: hello
```

**Explanation:**

*   `Foo`: This refers to the type of the class `Foo` (specifically, the type of instances of `Foo`).
*   `['ReactProperties']`: This is the indexed access operator. When used in a type context, it looks up the type of the property named `ReactProperties` on the `Foo` type.

This is a very common and powerful pattern in TypeScript for creating new types based on existing ones, promoting code reuse and type safety.

