Okay, Flexbox is perfect for this! Here are the most common and effective ways to achieve this:

**Method 1: `justify-content: space-between;` (Simplest for two items)**

This is the most straightforward method when you have exactly two items (or groups of items) and you want them at opposite ends.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Flex Buttons</title>
<style>
  .button-container {
    display: flex; /* Enables flexbox */
    justify-content: space-between; /* Pushes items to opposite ends */
    align-items: center; /* Optional: vertically aligns items in the center */
    padding: 10px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc; /* Just for visualization */
    width: 100%; /* Or any width you desire */
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  .button {
    padding: 10px 20px;
    border: none;
    color: white;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .button-left {
    background-color: #007bff; /* Blue */
  }

  .button-right {
    background-color: #28a745; /* Green */
  }

  /* Responsive considerations (example) */
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .button-container {
      flex-direction: column; /* Stack buttons vertically on small screens */
      align-items: stretch; /* Make buttons full width when stacked */
    }
    .button {
      margin-bottom: 10px; /* Add space between stacked buttons */
    }
    .button:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

  <div class="button-container">
    <button class="button button-left">Left Button</button>
    <button class="button button-right">Right Button</button>
  </div>

</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**
*   `display: flex;` on the `.button-container` turns it into a flex container.
*   `justify-content: space-between;` distributes the space *between* the flex items. The first item is pushed to the start, and the last item is pushed to the end.
*   `align-items: center;` (optional) ensures the buttons are vertically centered if they have different heights or if the container has a fixed height.

---

**Method 2: Using `margin: auto;` on one of the buttons**

This method is very flexible, especially if you have more than two items and want to group some on the left and one (or a group) on the right.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Flex Buttons - Margin Auto</title>
<style>
  .button-container-margin {
    display: flex; /* Enables flexbox */
    align-items: center; /* Optional: vertically aligns items */
    padding: 10px;
    border: 1px solid #ddd; /* Just for visualization */
    width: 100%;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  .button {
    padding: 10px 20px;
    border: none;
    color: white;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .button-left {
    background-color: #007bff; /* Blue */
    /* No specific margin needed, it will stay left by default */
  }

  .button-right-margin {
    background-color: #dc3545; /* Red */
    margin-left: auto; /* THIS IS THE KEY! */
  }

  /* Example with a third button in the middle */
  .button-container-margin-extra {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 10px;
    border: 1px solid #aaa;
    margin-top: 20px;
  }
  .button-middle {
    background-color: #ffc107; /* Yellow */
    margin-left: 10px; /* Space from left button */
  }
  .button-right-margin-extra {
    background-color: #17a2b8; /* Teal */
    margin-left: auto; /* Pushes this to the far right */
  }

  /* Responsive considerations (same as above) */
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .button-container-margin, .button-container-margin-extra {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
    }
    .button {
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }
    .button:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
    .button-right-margin, .button-right-margin-extra {
      margin-left: 0; /* Reset margin-left when stacked */
    }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

  <p>Using margin-left: auto on the right button:</p>
  <div class="button-container-margin">
    <button class="button button-left">Left Button</button>
    <button class="button button-right-margin">Right Button</button>
  </div>

  <p>Example with a third button:</p>
  <div class="button-container-margin-extra">
    <button class="button button-left">Left 1</button>
    <button class="button button-middle">Middle</button>
    <button class="button button-right-margin-extra">Rightmost</button>
  </div>

</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**
*   `display: flex;` on the container.
*   `margin-left: auto;` on the right-aligned button tells it to take up all available horizontal space to its left, effectively pushing it to the right.
*   If you wanted the first button right-aligned and the second left-aligned (unusual, but possible), you'd put `margin-right: auto;` on the first button.

---

**Method 3: Using a "Spacer" Element (less common for just two items, but useful for complex layouts)**

You can insert an empty element that grows to fill the space.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Flex Buttons - Spacer</title>
<style>
  .button-container-spacer {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 10px;
    border: 1px solid #eee;
    width: 100%;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  .button {
    padding: 10px 20px;
    border: none;
    color: white;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .button-left {
    background-color: #007bff;
  }

  .button-right {
    background-color: #6f42c1; /* Purple */
  }

  .spacer {
    flex-grow: 1; /* This element will take up all available space */
  }

  /* Responsive considerations (same as above) */
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .button-container-spacer {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
    }
    .button {
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }
    .button:last-child {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
    .spacer {
      display: none; /* Hide spacer when stacked */
    }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

  <div class="button-container-spacer">
    <button class="button button-left">Left Button</button>
    <div class="spacer"></div>
    <button class="button button-right">Right Button</button>
  </div>

</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**
*   The `<div class="spacer"></div>` is an empty flex item.
*   `flex-grow: 1;` on the spacer makes it expand to fill any available space along the main axis.

---

**Which to choose?**

*   **For exactly two items:** `justify-content: space-between;` (Method 1) is usually the cleanest and most semantic.
*   **For more complex layouts or when you want more control over one side:** `margin: auto;` (Method 2) is very powerful and flexible. For instance, if you have multiple items on the left and one on the right.
*   **Spacer element (Method 3):** Can be useful, but for just two buttons, it adds an extra HTML element which might be unnecessary. It's more common when you need to visually separate groups of elements.

All these methods leverage Flexbox's responsive nature. The buttons will stay aligned as the container resizes, and you can easily change `flex-direction` (e.g., to `column`) for smaller screens using media queries, as shown in the examples.

Okay, there are several ways to calculate Pi (π) in JavaScript, ranging from the extremely simple (using the built-in constant) to more complex algorithmic approaches.

Here are a few methods:

**1. Using the Built-in `Math.PI` (Recommended for most cases)**

This is the easiest and most accurate way for practical purposes, as it uses a high-precision, pre-calculated value.

```javascript
console.log("Using Math.PI:");
console.log(Math.PI); // Output: 3.141592653589793
```

**2. Leibniz Formula (Gregory-Leibniz series)**

This is a simple infinite series, but it converges very slowly.
Formula: `π/4 = 1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + 1/9 - ...`

```javascript
function calculatePiLeibniz(iterations) {
  let piApproximation = 0;
  let sign = 1; // To alternate between adding and subtracting

  for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
    let term = 1 / (2 * i + 1);
    piApproximation += sign * term;
    sign *= -1; // Flip the sign for the next term
  }
  return 4 * piApproximation;
}

console.log("\nUsing Leibniz Formula:");
let iterationsLeibniz = 100000; // More iterations = better accuracy (but slower)
console.log(`With ${iterationsLeibniz} iterations: ${calculatePiLeibniz(iterationsLeibniz)}`);
// Try with more iterations, e.g., 1,000,000, to see it get closer

// Example:
// With 100000 iterations: 3.1415826535897198 (Still not super accurate)
```

**3. Nilakantha Series**

This series converges much faster than the Leibniz formula.
Formula: `π = 3 + 4/(2*3*4) - 4/(4*5*6) + 4/(6*7*8) - 4/(8*9*10) + ...`

```javascript
function calculatePiNilakantha(iterations) {
  let piApproximation = 3.0;
  let sign = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
    let n = 2 + (i * 2); // Denominator starts with 2, then 4, then 6...
    let term = 4.0 / (n * (n + 1) * (n + 2));
    piApproximation += sign * term;
    sign *= -1;
  }
  return piApproximation;
}

console.log("\nUsing Nilakantha Series:");
let iterationsNilakantha = 1000; // Converges much faster
console.log(`With ${iterationsNilakantha} iterations: ${calculatePiNilakantha(iterationsNilakantha)}`);
// Example:
// With 1000 iterations: 3.1415926535897915 (Very close!)
```

**4. Monte Carlo Method (Dartboard Method)**

This is a probabilistic method.
Imagine a square with a side length of 2 units (from -1 to 1 on x and y axes).
Inside this square, inscribe a circle with a radius of 1 unit.
- Area of the square = (2*r)^2 = (2*1)^2 = 4
- Area of the circle = π * r^2 = π * 1^2 = π

If you randomly throw darts at the square, the ratio of darts landing inside the circle to the total number of darts thrown will approximate the ratio of the areas:
`(Darts in Circle) / (Total Darts) ≈ (Area of Circle) / (Area of Square)`
`(Darts in Circle) / (Total Darts) ≈ π / 4`
So, `π ≈ 4 * (Darts in Circle) / (Total Darts)`

```javascript
function calculatePiMonteCarlo(numPoints) {
  let pointsInCircle = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < numPoints; i++) {
    // Generate random x and y coordinates between -1 and 1
    let x = Math.random() * 2 - 1;
    let y = Math.random() * 2 - 1;

    // Check if the point (x, y) is inside the unit circle (x^2 + y^2 <= 1)
    if (x * x + y * y <= 1) {
      pointsInCircle++;
    }
  }
  return 4 * (pointsInCircle / numPoints);
}

console.log("\nUsing Monte Carlo Method:");
let numPointsMonteCarlo = 1000000; // More points = better accuracy (but slower)
console.log(`With ${numPointsMonteCarlo} random points: ${calculatePiMonteCarlo(numPointsMonteCarlo)}`);
// Example (will vary due to randomness):
// With 1000000 random points: 3.141868
```

**Which one to choose?**

*   **For almost all practical applications: `Math.PI` is the best.** It's fast, accurate, and built-in.
*   **For learning or demonstration:** The Leibniz, Nilakantha, or Monte Carlo methods are interesting ways to see how Pi can be approximated algorithmically. Nilakantha converges much faster than Leibniz. Monte Carlo is a neat example of probabilistic algorithms.

**How to run this code:**

1.  Open your web browser's developer console (usually by pressing F12).
2.  Go to the "Console" tab.
3.  Copy and paste the JavaScript code snippets into the console and press Enter.
4.  Alternatively, save the code as an `.html` file with `<script>` tags or a `.js` file and run it with Node.js (`node yourfile.js`).

```html
