**Explanation:**

*   **`Sidebar.js`:**
    *   Contains the actual navigation links.
    *   Uses `fixed inset-y-0 left-0 z-40` to be positioned on the left.
    *   `w-64` sets its width.
    *   **Key for animation:**
        *   `transition-transform duration-300 ease-in-out`: Enables smooth transition for the `transform` property.
        *   `md:translate-x-0`: On medium screens and up, it's always visible (`translate-x-0`).
        *   `mobileMenuOpen ? 'translate-x-0' : '-translate-x-full'`: On smaller screens, it slides in (`translate-x-0`) if `mobileMenuOpen` is true, otherwise it's hidden off-screen to the left (`-translate-x-full`).
    *   An overlay (`fixed inset-0 z-30 bg-gray-900/80 md:hidden`) is shown when the mobile menu is open. Clicking it closes the menu.
    *   A close button (`XMarkIcon`) is also provided within the mobile sidebar.

*   **`Layout.js` (or `app/layout.js`):**
    *   Manages the `mobileMenuOpen` state.
    *   Renders the `Sidebar` and passes the state and setter function to it.
    *   Renders a hamburger button (`Bars3Icon`) that is `md:hidden` (visible only on small screens). Clicking it sets `mobileMenuOpen` to `true`.
    *   The `main` content area has `md:pl-64`. This padding on the left for medium screens and up prevents the fixed desktop sidebar from overlapping the content. On small screens, this padding doesn't apply, so content takes full width.
    *   The `useEffect` hook for `document.body.style.overflow` is a common pattern to prevent the page content from scrolling when a modal or off-canvas menu is open.

*   **Tailwind Classes:**
    *   `fixed`, `sticky`: For positioning.
    *   `inset-y-0`, `left-0`, `top-0`: For positioning.
    *   `z-10, z-20, z-30, z-40`: For stacking order.
    *   `w-64`, `h-16`, `h-screen`: For dimensions.
    *   `md:hidden`, `md:block`, `md:translate-x-0`: Responsive prefixes. `md:` applies styles from the "medium" breakpoint upwards.
    *   `transform`, `translate-x-0`, `-translate-x-full`: For moving elements.
    *   `transition-transform`, `duration-300`, `ease-in-out`: For smooth animations.
    *   `flex`, `items-center`, `justify-between`, etc.: For flexbox layout.

Now, when you view your app:
*   **Desktop:** The sidebar will be permanently visible on the left.
*   **Mobile/Small Screens:** The sidebar will be hidden. A hamburger icon will appear in the top bar. Tapping it will slide the sidebar in from the left, overlaying the content. Tapping the overlay or the "X" button will slide it back out.

instruction:
help me remove column A  based on this code data vertical3;
	set vertical2;
format Treatment $Drug. Effectiveness $Effective. Sex $Sex. ;

output:
Okay, to remove column `A` when creating `vertical3` from `vertical2`, you have a couple of good options within your DATA step.

The `FORMAT` statement only changes how data is displayed or written; it doesn't add or remove columns.

Here are the common ways to remove column `A`:

1.  **Using the `DROP` statement:**
    This is the most straightforward way to explicitly state which columns you want to exclude from the new dataset.

    ```sas
    data vertical3;
        set vertical2;
        drop A; /* This line removes column A */
        format Treatment $Drug. Effectiveness $Effective. Sex $Sex.;
    run;
    ```

2.  **Using the `DROP=` dataset option on the `SET` statement:**
    This tells SAS to drop the column as it's being read from `vertical2`, before it even fully enters the program data vector for the `vertical3` step. This can sometimes be slightly more efficient.

    ```sas
    data vertical3;
        set vertical2 (drop=A); /* Column A is dropped as vertical2 is read */
        format Treatment $Drug. Effectiveness $Effective. Sex $Sex.;
    run;
    ```

**Which one to choose?**
*   Both achieve the same result: `vertical3` will not contain column `A`.
*   The `DROP` statement is very clear and explicit.
*   The `DROP=` dataset option is often preferred for its conciseness and potential (though often minor) efficiency gain, especially if you're dropping many columns from an input dataset.

**If you wanted to keep only specific columns (and implicitly drop all others), you could use `KEEP`:**

```sas
/* Example: If vertical2 had columns A, B, C, Treatment, Effectiveness, Sex
   and you only wanted B, C, and the formatted ones: */
data vertical3;
    set vertical2;
    keep B C Treatment Effectiveness Sex; /* Only these columns will be in vertical3 */
    format Treatment $Drug. Effectiveness $Effective. Sex $Sex.;
run;

/* Or using the KEEP= dataset option */
data vertical3;
    set vertical2 (keep=B C Treatment Effectiveness Sex);
    format Treatment $Drug. Effectiveness $Effective. Sex $Sex.;
run;
```
But for just removing one or a few specific columns, `DROP` or `DROP=` is more direct.

**In your case, to simply remove column A, either of the first two options will work perfectly.** I'd probably lean towards the `DROP=` dataset option for this scenario:

```sas
data vertical3;
    set vertical2 (drop=A);
    format Treatment $Drug. Effectiveness $Effective. Sex $Sex.;
run;
```

Okay, let's create a simple iMessage extension that displays two buttons in the compact (keyboard) view.

**What this extension will do:**
*   When you open the iMessage app extension, it will show two buttons.
*   Tapping these buttons will print a message to the Xcode console (for demonstration). You can later make them send messages or perform other actions.

---

**Steps:**

1.  **Create a New Xcode Project:**
    *   Open Xcode.
    *   Choose "Create a new Xcode project."
    *   Select the "iOS" tab.
    *   Choose "iMessage App" and click "Next."
    *   Product Name: `TwoButtonKeyboard` (or whatever you like).
    *   Team: Your development team.
    *   Language: Swift.
    *   User Interface: Storyboard (though we'll do most UI in code for simplicity here).
    *   Uncheck "Include Tests" if you don't need them for this example.
    *   Click "Next" and choose a location to save your project.

2.  **Understanding the Project Structure:**
    *   You'll see two targets:
        *   `TwoButtonKeyboard` (the main app, which is just a container for the extension).
        *   `MessagesExtension` (this is where your iMessage app logic goes).
    *   Focus on the `MessagesExtension` group.

3.  **Modify `MessagesViewController.swift`:**
    This file is the main view controller for your iMessage extension.

    ```swift
    import UIKit
    import Messages

    class MessagesViewController: MSMessagesAppViewController {

        // Declare buttons as properties so they can be accessed
        let button1 = UIButton(type: .system)
        let button2 = UIButton(type: .system)
        let stackView = UIStackView()

        override func viewDidLoad() {
            super.viewDidLoad()
            // Do any additional setup after loading the view.
            setupUI()
        }

        func setupUI() {
            // Configure Button 1
            button1.setTitle("Action 1", for: .normal)
            button1.addTarget(self, action: #selector(didTapButton1), for: .touchUpInside)
            button1.backgroundColor = .systemBlue
            button1.setTitleColor(.white, for: .normal)
            button1.layer.cornerRadius = 8
            button1.contentEdgeInsets = UIEdgeInsets(top: 10, left: 15, bottom: 10, right: 15)


            // Configure Button 2
            button2.setTitle("Action 2", for: .normal)
            button2.addTarget(self, action: #selector(didTapButton2), for: .touchUpInside)
            button2.backgroundColor = .systemGreen
            button2.setTitleColor(.white, for: .normal)
            button2.layer.cornerRadius = 8
            button2.contentEdgeInsets = UIEdgeInsets(top: 10, left: 15, bottom: 10, right: 15)

            // Configure Stack View
            stackView.axis = .horizontal
            stackView.distribution = .fillEqually // Makes buttons same width
            stackView.alignment = .center
            stackView.spacing = 10
            stackView.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false // Important for Auto Layout

            // Add buttons to stack view
            stackView.addArrangedSubview(button1)
            stackView.addArrangedSubview(button2)

            // Add stack view to the view controller's view
            view.addSubview(stackView)

            // Setup Auto Layout constraints for the stack view
            NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
                stackView.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.leadingAnchor, constant: 20),
                stackView.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.trailingAnchor, constant: -20),
                stackView.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.centerYAnchor), // Center vertically
                // Optionally, set a height for the buttons or stack view if needed
                // button1.heightAnchor.constraint(equalToConstant: 44),
                // button2.heightAnchor.constraint(equalToConstant: 44)
            ])
        }

        // MARK: - Button Actions

        @objc func didTapButton1() {
            print("Button 1 Tapped!")
            // Here you could, for example, send a message:
            // if let conversation = activeConversation {
            //     let layout = MSMessageTemplateLayout()
            //     layout.caption = "Sent from Button 1"
            //     let message = MSMessage()
            //     message.layout = layout
            //     conversation.insert(message, completionHandler: nil)
            // }
            // Or request presentation style change
            // requestPresentationStyle(.expanded)
        }

        @objc func didTapButton2() {
            print("Button 2 Tapped!")
            // Similar actions as button 1
        }

        // MARK: - MSMessagesAppViewController Overrides

        override func willBecomeActive(with conversation: MSConversation) {
            super.willBecomeActive(with: conversation)
            // Called when the extension is about to move from the inactive to active state.
            // This will happen when the user taps your extension icon.
            // Ensure we present in compact mode (keyboard view).
            presentViewController(for: conversation, with: self.presentationStyle)
        }

        override func didResignActive(with conversation: MSConversation) {
            // Called when the extension is about to move from the active to inactive state.
            // This will happen when the user dismisses the extension, navigates to another
            // conversation, or chooses another extension.
        }

        override func didReceive(_ message: MSMessage, conversation: MSConversation) {
            // Called when a message arrives that was generated by another instance of this
            // extension on a remote device.
        }

        override func didStartSending(_ message: MSMessage, conversation: MSConversation) {
            // Called when the user taps the send button.
        }

        override func didCancelSending(_ message: MSMessage, conversation: MSConversation) {
            // Called when the user deletes the message without sending it.
        }

        override func willTransition(to presentationStyle: MSMessagesAppPresentationStyle) {
            // Called before the extension transitions to a new presentation style.
            // Use this method to prepare for the change in presentation style.
            // For example, you might hide/show UI elements based on the style.
            print("Will transition to: \(presentationStyle == .compact ? "Compact" : "Expanded")")
        }

        override func didTransition(to presentationStyle: MSMessagesAppPresentationStyle) {
            // Called after the extension transitions to a new presentation style.
            // Use this method to finalize any changes based on the new presentation style.
            print("Did transition to: \(presentationStyle == .compact ? "Compact" : "Expanded")")
        }

        // This private method is an example of how to switch presentation styles.
        // You would call this from a button tap or other event.
        private func presentViewController(for conversation: MSConversation, with presentationStyle: MSMessagesAppPresentationStyle) {
            // Determine the controller to present.
            // For this simple example, we always use `self`.
            // In more complex apps, you might have different view controllers for compact and expanded modes.

            // Remove any existing child view controllers
            // This is important if you switch between different VCs for different styles
            for child in children {
                child.willMove(toParent: nil)
                child.view.removeFromSuperview()
                child.removeFromParent()
            }

            // Add self as child (if not already)
            // addChild(self) // Not strictly needed if always using self and not removing
            // view.addSubview(self.view) // Not strictly needed if always using self's view

            // Ensure the view is laid out correctly if you were adding a new child VC's view
            // self.view.frame = self.view.bounds
            // self.view.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
            // NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            //     self.view.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.leadingAnchor),
            //     self.view.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.trailingAnchor),
            //     self.view.topAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.topAnchor),
            //     self.view.bottomAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.bottomAnchor)
            // ])

            // The setupUI() method in viewDidLoad will handle the button display for compact mode.
            // If you had different UIs for compact and expanded, you'd adjust here.
            if presentationStyle == .compact {
                // UI for compact mode is already set up by viewDidLoad -> setupUI()
                print("Presenting in Compact Mode")
            } else if presentationStyle == .expanded {
                // You could set up a different UI for expanded mode here
                print("Presenting in Expanded Mode")
                // For example, show more content, different buttons, etc.
            }
        }
    }
    ```

4.  **Run the Extension:**
    *   Select the `MessagesExtension` scheme in Xcode (it should be selected by default).
    *   Choose a Simulator (e.g., iPhone 15 Pro) or a connected iOS device.
    *   Click the "Run" button (the play icon).
    *   Xcode will ask you to choose an app to run. Select "Messages."
    *   Once the Messages app loads on the simulator/device:
        *   Open any conversation (or start a new one).
        *   Tap the "App Store" icon next to the text input field.
        *   You should see your "TwoButtonKeyboard" app icon in the app drawer. If it's not enabled, tap "More (...)" and enable it.
        *   Tap your app's icon.
        *   Your two buttons should appear in the keyboard area.
        *   Tapping them will print to the Xcode console.

---

**Explanation of Key Parts:**

*   **`MSMessagesAppViewController`:** The base class for your iMessage extension's main view controller.
*   **`viewDidLoad()`:** Standard iOS method. We call `setupUI()` here.
*   **`setupUI()`:**
    *   Creates two `UIButton` instances.
    *   Sets their titles and adds target-action methods (`didTapButton1`, `didTapButton2`).
    *   Uses a `UIStackView` for easy horizontal layout of the buttons.
    *   `translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false` is crucial when using Auto Layout programmatically.
    *   `NSLayoutConstraint.activate([...])` sets up the constraints to position the stack view (and thus the buttons) in the center of the view, with some padding.
*   **`@objc func didTapButton...()`:** These are the methods called when the buttons are tapped.
*   **`willBecomeActive(with:)`:** This is where you typically decide which presentation style (`.compact` or `.expanded`) your extension should start in. For this request, we want the keyboard view, which is `.compact`. The `presentViewController(for:with:)` method is called to manage the presentation.
*   **`presentViewController(for:with:)`:** This method is responsible for displaying the correct view controller for the given presentation style. In our simple case, `self` (the `MessagesViewController`) is always used, and `setupUI` in `viewDidLoad` handles the compact view. If you had different view controllers or UIs for compact vs. expanded, this is where you'd manage that transition.
*   **`presentationStyle`:** This property of `MSMessagesAppViewController` tells you the current style. It can be:
    *   `.compact`: The keyboard-sized view.
    *   `.expanded`: A taller, more full-screen view.
*   **Sending Messages (Commented Out):** The commented-out code in `didTapButton1` shows a very basic example of how you could create and insert an `MSMessage` into the conversation.

This provides a solid foundation. You can now expand on this by making the buttons send actual `MSMessage` objects, transition to an expanded view, or load more complex UIs.

Okay, you can use JavaScript in the browser's developer console to fetch the content of a URL and then trigger a download of that content.

**However, there's a very important caveat: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS).**

*   **If the URL you want to download is on a *different domain* than the page where you have the console open,** the `fetch` request will likely be blocked by the browser's CORS policy unless the target server explicitly allows requests from your current origin (which is unlikely for arbitrary websites).
*   **The easiest workaround for CORS is to first navigate your browser to the exact URL you want to download. Then, open the developer console on *that page* and run the script. In this case, the script will be running on the same origin, and CORS won't be an issue.**

Here's the JavaScript code you can paste into the console:

```javascript
async function downloadPageAsHTML(url, filename = 'downloaded_page.html') {
    try {
        console.log(`Fetching ${url}...`);

        // Fetch the page content
        // This fetch is subject to CORS. If 'url' is a different origin, it might fail.
        const response = await fetch(url, {
            headers: {
                // Some sites might require an Accept header for HTML
                'Accept': 'text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,image/webp,*/*;q=0.8'
            }
        });

        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status} - ${response.statusText}`);
        }

        const htmlContent = await response.text();
        console.log('Content fetched successfully.');

        // Create a Blob with the HTML content
        const blob = new Blob([htmlContent], { type: 'text/html;charset=utf-8' });

        // Create a link element
        const link = document.createElement('a');

        // Create an object URL for the Blob
        link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);

        // Set the download attribute with the desired filename
        link.download = filename;

        // Append the link to the body (required for Firefox to trigger download reliably)
        document.body.appendChild(link);

        // Programmatically click the link to trigger the download
        link.click();

        // Remove the link from the document
        document.body.removeChild(link);

        // Revoke the object URL to free up resources
        URL.revokeObjectURL(link.href);

        console.log(`Download initiated as ${filename}`);

    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error downloading page:', error);
        alert(`Failed to download page. Check the console for details.
Common reasons:
1. CORS policy: The server at '${url}' might not allow requests from this origin.
   WORKAROUND: Open the target URL (${url}) in a new tab, THEN open the console on THAT page and run this script.
2. Network error or invalid URL.`);
    }
}

// --- How to use ---
// 1. Open the Developer Console (usually F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I / Cmd+Option+I).
// 2. Go to the "Console" tab.
// 3. Paste the entire function code above (from `async function...` down to the closing `}`) and press Enter.
//    This defines the function.
// 4. Then, call the function with the URL you want to download and an optional filename:
//
//    Examples:
//
//    a) To download a specific page (CORS WORKAROUND MIGHT BE NEEDED):
//       downloadPageAsHTML('https://www.example.com/somepage.html', 'example_page.html');
//
//    b) To download the *current* page you are on (NO CORS issue):
//       downloadPageAsHTML(window.location.href, 'current_page.html');
//
//    c) To download the current page with a filename derived from its title:
//       let currentTitle = document.title.replace(/[^a-z0-9]/gi, '_').toLowerCase() || 'page';
//       downloadPageAsHTML(window.location.href, currentTitle + '.html');

// --- IMPORTANT NOTE ON CORS ---
// If you are trying to download a page from a *different website* than the one
// where you have the console open, this script will likely fail due to
// Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) policies.
//
// THE BEST WORKAROUND:
// 1. Navigate your browser directly to the URL you want to download.
// 2. Once the page is loaded, open the Developer Console (F12) on *that* page.
// 3. Paste and run this script. You can then use:
//    downloadPageAsHTML(window.location.href, 'mypage.html');
//    (or provide the full URL again, it will now be same-origin).
```

**How to use it:**

1.  **Navigate (if necessary):** If you want to download `https://example.com/article1`, first go to `https://example.com/article1` in your browser.
2.  **Open Developer Console:**
    *   Windows/Linux: `F12` or `Ctrl+Shift+I`
    *   Mac: `Cmd+Option+I`
3.  **Go to the "Console" tab.**
4.  **Paste the Code:** Copy the entire `async function downloadPageAsHTML(...) { ... }` block and paste it into the console. Press `Enter`. This defines the function.
5.  **Call the Function:** Now, type one of the following commands and press `Enter`:

    *   To download the page you are currently viewing:
        ```javascript
        downloadPageAsHTML(window.location.href, 'my_current_page.html');
        ```
    *   Or, if you're on `example.com` and want to download `example.com/otherpage` (same origin):
        ```javascript
        downloadPageAsHTML('https://example.com/otherpage', 'other_page.html');
        ```
    *   If you insist on trying a cross-origin download (which will likely fail due to CORS, but you're on `somesite.com` and want `othersite.com`):
        ```javascript
        downloadPageAsHTML('https://othersite.com/page-to-download.html', 'othersite_page.html');
        ```
        (You'll likely see a CORS error in the console if you do this from a different origin).

The browser should then prompt you to save the HTML file.

Okay, here's a Rust program that does this.

First, make sure you have `yt-dlp` installed and accessible in your system's PATH. You can check by running `yt-dlp --version` in your terminal.

**Project Setup:**

1.  Create a new Rust project:
    ```bash
    cargo new youtube_downloader
    cd youtube_downloader
    ```

2.  Add the `url` crate to your `Cargo.toml` for robust URL parsing:
    ```toml
    [dependencies]
    url = "2.5.0" # Check for the latest version on crates.io
    ```

**Rust Code (`src/main.rs`):**

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{self, BufRead, BufReader};
use std::path::Path;
use std::process::Command;
use std::env;
use url::Url; // Import the Url type

// Define a custom error type to handle various errors
#[derive(Debug)]
enum AppError {
    Io(io::Error),
    UrlParse(url::ParseError),
    YtDlpNotFound,
    YtDlpFailed(String),
    NoInputFile,
}

// Implement From for io::Error to easily convert
impl From<io::Error> for AppError {
    fn from(err: io::Error) -> AppError {
        AppError::Io(err)
    }
}

// Implement From for url::ParseError
impl From<url::ParseError> for AppError {
    fn from(err: url::ParseError) -> AppError {
        AppError::UrlParse(err)
    }
}

impl std::fmt::Display for AppError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
        match self {
            AppError::Io(err) => write!(f, "IO Error: {}", err),
            AppError::UrlParse(err) => write!(f, "URL Parse Error: {}", err),
            AppError::YtDlpNotFound => write!(f, "yt-dlp command not found. Please ensure it's installed and in your PATH."),
            AppError::YtDlpFailed(msg) => write!(f, "yt-dlp execution failed: {}", msg),
            AppError::NoInputFile => write!(f, "No input file specified. Usage: youtube_downloader <filepath>"),
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for AppError {}


fn is_youtube_url(url_str: &str) -> Result<bool, url::ParseError> {
    match Url::parse(url_str) {
        Ok(parsed_url) => {
            if let Some(host) = parsed_url.host_str() {
                Ok(host == "youtube.com" || host == "www.youtube.com" || host == "youtu.be" || host == "music.youtube.com")
            } else {
                Ok(false) // No host, not a typical HTTP/S URL
            }
        }
        Err(e) => {
            // If parsing fails, it's likely not a valid URL, let alone a YouTube one.
            // However, we propagate the parse error in case the caller wants to handle it.
            // For this specific function's logic, we could also just return Ok(false).
            // But for better error context, let's propagate.
            println!("Warning: Could not parse URL '{}': {}", url_str, e);
            Err(e)
        }
    }
}

fn download_with_yt_dlp(url: &str, output_dir: &str) -> Result<(), AppError> {
    println!("Attempting to download: {}", url);

    // Ensure output directory exists
    std::fs::create_dir_all(output_dir)?;

    let output = Command::new("yt-dlp")
        .arg(url)
        .arg("-P") // Specify output directory prefix
        .arg(output_dir)
        .arg("-o") // Output template
        .arg("%(title)s [%(id)s].%(ext)s") // Example: Video Title [videoID].mp4
        // .arg("--quiet") // Uncomment for less output from yt-dlp
        // .arg("--progress") // Uncomment for progress bar
        .output(); // Use output() to capture stdout/stderr for better error reporting

    match output {
        Ok(output_result) => {
            if output_result.status.success() {
                println!("Successfully downloaded: {}", url);
                if !output_result.stdout.is_empty() {
                    // println!("yt-dlp stdout:\n{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(&output_result.stdout));
                }
                Ok(())
            } else {
                let stderr = String::from_utf8_lossy(&output_result.stderr);
                let stdout = String::from_utf8_lossy(&output_result.stdout);
                eprintln!("Failed to download {}. yt-dlp exited with status: {}", url, output_result.status);
                if !stderr.is_empty() {
                    eprintln!("yt-dlp stderr:\n{}", stderr);
                }
                if !stdout.is_empty() {
                     // yt-dlp often puts error details in stdout too
                    eprintln!("yt-dlp stdout:\n{}", stdout);
                }
                Err(AppError::YtDlpFailed(format!(
                    "URL: {}. Status: {}. Stderr: {}. Stdout: {}",
                    url, output_result.status, stderr, stdout
                )))
            }
        }
        Err(e) => {
            if e.kind() == io::ErrorKind::NotFound {
                Err(AppError::YtDlpNotFound)
            } else {
                Err(AppError::Io(e))
            }
        }
    }
}

fn main() -> Result<(), AppError> {
    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();
    if args.len() < 2 {
        return Err(AppError::NoInputFile);
    }
    let filepath = &args[1];
    let output_directory = "youtube_downloads"; // All downloads will go here

    println!("Reading URLs from: {}", filepath);
    println!("YouTube videos will be downloaded to: {}/", output_directory);


    let file = File::open(Path::new(filepath))?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    let mut youtube_urls = Vec::new();
    let mut other_urls = Vec::new();

    for line_result in reader.lines() {
        let line = line_result?.trim().to_string();
        if line.is_empty() || line.starts_with('#') { // Skip empty lines and comments
            continue;
        }

        match is_youtube_url(&line) {
            Ok(true) => youtube_urls.push(line),
            Ok(false) => other_urls.push(line.clone()), // Clone because line is used in error msg
            Err(_) => {
                // is_youtube_url already prints a warning for parse errors
                // We'll categorize unparseable URLs as "other" for now
                other_urls.push(line);
            }
        }
    }

    println!("\n--- YouTube URLs to download ---");
    if youtube_urls.is_empty() {
        println!("No YouTube URLs found.");
    } else {
        for url in &youtube_urls {
            println!("{}", url);
        }
        println!("------------------------------\n");

        for url in youtube_urls {
            if let Err(e) = download_with_yt_dlp(&url, output_directory) {
                eprintln!("Error downloading {}: {}", url, e);
                // Decide if you want to continue with other downloads or stop
                // For now, it continues
            }
            println!("---"); // Separator between downloads
        }
    }

    println!("\n--- Other URLs ---");
    if other_urls.is_empty() {
        println!("No other URLs found.");
    } else {
        for url in other_urls {
            println!("{}", url);
        }
    }
    println!("------------------");

    Ok(())
}
```

**How to Use:**

1.  **Create a file** (e.g., `urls.txt`) with one URL per line:
    ```
    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ
    https://github.com/
    https://youtu.be/jNQXAC9IVRw
    https://www.rust-lang.org
    # This is a comment
    https://music.youtube.com/watch?v=L_jWHffIx5E
    https://invalid url format
    ```

2.  **Compile and run the program:**
    ```bash
    cargo build
    ./target/debug/youtube_downloader urls.txt
    ```
    Or, for a release build (optimized):
    ```bash
    cargo build --release
    ./target/release/youtube_downloader urls.txt
    ```

3.  The program will:
    *   Read `urls.txt`.
    *   Identify YouTube URLs.
    *   Attempt to download each YouTube URL into a directory named `youtube_downloads` (created in the same location where you run the executable).
    *   Print out the list of non-YouTube URLs.
    *   Print errors if `yt-dlp` fails or isn't found.

**Key Features and Explanations:**

*   **`url` Crate:** Used for more reliable parsing and host checking of URLs. Simple string `contains` can be fooled.
*   **`std::process::Command`:** Executes the `yt-dlp` command.
    *   `-P <directory>`: `yt-dlp` option to specify the output directory.
    *   `-o "%(title)s [%(id)s].%(ext)s"`: `yt-dlp` output template to name files nicely (e.g., "Video Title [videoId].mp4").
*   **Error Handling:**
    *   Uses a custom `AppError` enum to wrap different kinds of errors (I/O, URL parsing, `yt-dlp` issues).
    *   Implements `From` traits for easier conversion of standard errors into `AppError`.
    *   Implements `std::fmt::Display` and `std::error::Error` for `AppError` for better error reporting.
    *   The `?` operator is used extensively for concise error propagation.
*   **File Reading:** Uses `BufReader` for efficient line-by-line reading.
*   **Output Directory:** Creates a `youtube_downloads` directory if it doesn't exist.
*   **Robustness:** Skips empty lines and lines starting with `#` (comments). Handles potential URL parsing errors gracefully.
*   **`yt-dlp` Error Reporting:** Captures `stdout` and `stderr` from `yt-dlp` to provide more detailed error messages if a download fails.

This program provides a solid foundation. You could extend it further with features like concurrent downloads, more `yt-dlp` options configurable via command-line arguments, etc.

Okay, creating a true, infinitely recursive Sierpinski triangle directly in pure XAML is not feasible because XAML is a declarative language, not a procedural one, and doesn't have built-in recursion for drawing.

However, we can achieve this by:
1.  **Using C# code-behind** to programmatically generate the triangle segments and add them to a XAML `Canvas`. This is the most common and practical approach.
2.  (Less practical for deep recursion) Manually defining a few levels of the triangle in XAML, which would be very verbose and not truly fractal.

Let's go with **Option 1 (C# code-behind with a XAML Canvas)**. This example will be for WPF, but the concept is similar for UWP, Avalonia, or MAUI.

**Steps:**

1.  **Create a new WPF Application project.**
    *   In Visual Studio: File -> New -> Project -> Search for "WPF" -> Select "WPF Application" (for .NET Framework or .NET Core/.NET 5+).

2.  **Modify `MainWindow.xaml`:**
    We'll add a `Canvas` to draw on and perhaps a `Slider` to control the recursion depth.

    ```xml
    <Window x:Class="SierpinskiWpf.MainWindow"
            xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
            xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
            xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
            xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
            xmlns:local="clr-namespace:SierpinskiWpf"
            mc:Ignorable="d"
            Title="Sierpinski Triangle" Height="600" Width="800"
            Loaded="Window_Loaded" SizeChanged="Window_SizeChanged">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="5">
                <TextBlock Text="Depth:" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,5,0"/>
                <Slider x:Name="DepthSlider" Minimum="0" Maximum="8" Value="5" Width="200"
                        ValueChanged="DepthSlider_ValueChanged" TickFrequency="1" IsSnapToTickEnabled="True"/>
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding ElementName=DepthSlider, Path=Value}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="5,0,0,0"/>
                <TextBlock Text="Color:" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="20,0,5,0"/>
                <ComboBox x:Name="ColorComboBox" SelectedIndex="0" SelectionChanged="ColorComboBox_SelectionChanged" Width="100">
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="Black"/>
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="Blue"/>
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="Red"/>
                    <ComboBoxItem Content="Green"/>
                </ComboBox>
            </StackPanel>

            <Canvas x:Name="DrawingCanvas" Grid.Row="1" Background="LightGray"/>
        </Grid>
    </Window>
    ```

3.  **Modify `MainWindow.xaml.cs` (Code-Behind):**
    This is where the logic for drawing the triangle will reside.

    ```csharp
    using System.Windows;
    using System.Windows.Controls;
    using System.Windows.Media;
    using System.Windows.Shapes;

    namespace SierpinskiWpf
    {
        public partial class MainWindow : Window
        {
            private SolidColorBrush _triangleBrush = Brushes.Black;

            public MainWindow()
            {
                InitializeComponent();
            }

            private void Window_Loaded(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
            {
                Draw();
            }

            private void Window_SizeChanged(object sender, SizeChangedEventArgs e)
            {
                Draw(); // Redraw when window size changes
            }

            private void DepthSlider_ValueChanged(object sender, RoutedPropertyChangedEventArgs<double> e)
            {
                if (DrawingCanvas != null) // Ensure canvas is initialized
                {
                    Draw();
                }
            }
             private void ColorComboBox_SelectionChanged(object sender, SelectionChangedEventArgs e)
            {
                if (ColorComboBox.SelectedItem is ComboBoxItem selectedItem)
                {
                    switch (selectedItem.Content.ToString())
                    {
                        case "Black": _triangleBrush = Brushes.Black; break;
                        case "Blue": _triangleBrush = Brushes.Blue; break;
                        case "Red": _triangleBrush = Brushes.Red; break;
                        case "Green": _triangleBrush = Brushes.Green; break;
                        default: _triangleBrush = Brushes.Black; break;
                    }
                    Draw();
                }
            }

            private void Draw()
            {
                DrawingCanvas.Children.Clear(); // Clear previous drawing

                if (DrawingCanvas.ActualWidth == 0 || DrawingCanvas.ActualHeight == 0)
                    return; // Canvas not yet rendered

                double canvasWidth = DrawingCanvas.ActualWidth;
                double canvasHeight = DrawingCanvas.ActualHeight;
                int depth = (int)DepthSlider.Value;

                // Define the initial large triangle's vertices
                // Making it slightly smaller than the canvas for padding
                double padding = 20;
                Point p1 = new Point(canvasWidth / 2, padding);
                Point p2 = new Point(padding, canvasHeight - padding);
                Point p3 = new Point(canvasWidth - padding, canvasHeight - padding);

                DrawSierpinski(p1, p2, p3, depth);
            }

            private void DrawSierpinski(Point p1, Point p2, Point p3, int depth)
            {
                if (depth == 0)
                {
                    // Base case: draw the triangle
                    Polygon triangle = new Polygon();
                    triangle.Points.Add(p1);
                    triangle.Points.Add(p2);
                    triangle.Points.Add(p3);
                    triangle.Fill = _triangleBrush;
                    // triangle.Stroke = Brushes.DarkSlateGray; // Optional outline
                    // triangle.StrokeThickness = 0.5;
                    DrawingCanvas.Children.Add(triangle);
                }
                else
                {
                    // Recursive step:
                    // 1. Calculate midpoints of the sides
                    Point m12 = Midpoint(p1, p2); // Midpoint between p1 and p2
                    Point m23 = Midpoint(p2, p3); // Midpoint between p2 and p3
                    Point m31 = Midpoint(p3, p1); // Midpoint between p3 and p1

                    // 2. Recursively call DrawSierpinski for the three outer triangles
                    DrawSierpinski(p1, m12, m31, depth - 1);    // Top triangle
                    DrawSierpinski(m12, p2, m23, depth - 1);   // Bottom-left triangle
                    DrawSierpinski(m31, m23, p3, depth - 1);   // Bottom-right triangle
                }
            }

            private Point Midpoint(Point p1, Point p2)
            {
                return new Point((p1.X + p2.X) / 2, (p1.Y + p2.Y) / 2);
            }
        }
    }
    ```

**Explanation:**

1.  **`MainWindow.xaml`:**
    *   A `Grid` organizes a `StackPanel` (for controls) and the `DrawingCanvas`.
    *   `DepthSlider`: Allows the user to control the recursion depth of the Sierpinski triangle.
    *   `ColorComboBox`: Allows changing the fill color of the triangles.
    *   `DrawingCanvas`: This is where the triangles will be drawn. It has a `LightGray` background.
    *   `Window_Loaded` and `Window_SizeChanged`: Events that trigger the drawing/redrawing.

2.  **`MainWindow.xaml.cs`:**
    *   `_triangleBrush`: Stores the current brush for drawing triangles.
    *   `Window_Loaded()`: Calls `Draw()` when the window first loads.
    *   `Window_SizeChanged()`: Calls `Draw()` whenever the window is resized, making the triangle responsive.
    *   `DepthSlider_ValueChanged()`: Calls `Draw()` when the slider value changes.
    *   `ColorComboBox_SelectionChanged()`: Updates `_triangleBrush` and calls `Draw()`.
    *   `Draw()`:
        *   Clears any existing children from `DrawingCanvas`.
        *   Gets the current canvas dimensions and depth from the slider.
        *   Defines the three vertices (`p1`, `p2`, `p3`) of the outermost triangle. These are calculated to roughly fit the canvas with some padding.
        *   Calls the recursive `DrawSierpinski` function.
    *   `DrawSierpinski(Point p1, Point p2, Point p3, int depth)`:
        *   **Base Case (`depth == 0`):** Creates a `Polygon` shape, adds the three points to its `Points` collection, sets its `Fill` color, and adds it to the `DrawingCanvas.Children`.
        *   **Recursive Step (`depth > 0`):**
            *   Calculates the midpoints of the three sides of the current triangle (`m12`, `m23`, `m31`).
            *   Recursively calls `DrawSierpinski` three times, each time for one of the smaller "corner" triangles, and decrements the `depth`. The central triangle formed by the midpoints is skipped, which is the essence of the Sierpinski algorithm.
    *   `Midpoint(Point p1, Point p2)`: A helper function to calculate the midpoint between two points.

**How to Run:**
Simply build and run the WPF project. You should see a window with a slider. Adjust the slider to change the depth of the Sierpinski triangle. You can also change its color.

This method leverages XAML for the UI layout and controls, and C# for the dynamic and recursive drawing logic, which is a standard pattern in XAML-based applications.

Okay, here's a C# program that sends a POST request with a client certificate attached.

**Prerequisites:**

1.  **A Client Certificate:** You'll need a client certificate in a PFX (or P12) file format, which includes both the public certificate and its corresponding private key. You'll also need the password for this PFX file.
2.  **A Server Configured for Client Certificate Authentication:** The server you are sending the request to must be configured to request and validate client certificates. For testing, you might set up a local IIS, Nginx, or Apache server to require client certs.
3.  **.NET SDK:** Ensure you have the .NET SDK installed (e.g., .NET 6, 7, or 8).

**C# Code:**

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json; // For System.Text.Json (built-in with .NET Core/.NET 5+)
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class ClientCertificatePost
{
    public static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // --- Configuration ---
        string targetUrl = "https://your-api-endpoint.com/resource"; //  REPLACE with your target URL
        string pfxCertificatePath = "path/to/your/client_certificate.pfx"; // REPLACE with your PFX file path
        string pfxPassword = "your_pfx_password"; // REPLACE with your PFX password

        // Optional: For development/testing with self-signed SERVER certificates
        // Set to true if the SERVER you are connecting to uses a self-signed cert.
        // WARNING: DO NOT USE THIS IN PRODUCTION for public-facing servers.
        bool trustSelfSignedServerCert = false;

        // --- Validate PFX file exists ---
        if (!File.Exists(pfxCertificatePath))
        {
            Console.ForegroundColor = ConsoleColor.Red;
            Console.WriteLine($"Error: Certificate file not found at '{pfxCertificatePath}'");
            Console.ResetColor();
            Console.WriteLine("Please update the 'pfxCertificatePath' variable in the code.");
            return;
        }

        Console.WriteLine($"Attempting to POST to: {targetUrl}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Using client certificate: {pfxCertificatePath}");

        try
        {
            // 1. Load the client certificate
            // X509KeyStorageFlags.Exportable is often needed if the private key might be re-exported.
            // X509KeyStorageFlags.UserKeySet stores the key in the current user's profile.
            // Use X509KeyStorageFlags.MachineKeySet if the app runs under a service account that needs machine-level access.
            X509Certificate2 clientCertificate = new X509Certificate2(pfxCertificatePath, pfxPassword,
                                                                    X509KeyStorageFlags.UserKeySet | X509KeyStorageFlags.Exportable);
            Console.WriteLine($"Successfully loaded client certificate: {clientCertificate.Subject} (Expires: {clientCertificate.NotAfter})");

            // 2. Create HttpClientHandler and attach the certificate
            HttpClientHandler handler = new HttpClientHandler();
            handler.ClientCertificateOptions = ClientCertificateOption.Manual; // Important: tells handler to use certs from ClientCertificates collection
            handler.ClientCertificates.Add(clientCertificate);

            // (Optional) For development: Trust self-signed server certificates
            if (trustSelfSignedServerCert)
            {
                handler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = HttpClientHandler.DangerousAcceptAnyServerCertificateValidator;
                Console.ForegroundColor = ConsoleColor.Yellow;
                Console.WriteLine("WARNING: Trusting any server certificate. For development/testing ONLY.");
                Console.ResetColor();
            }

            // 3. Create HttpClient with the handler
            using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient(handler))
            {
                // 4. Prepare the POST request content (e.g., JSON)
                var payload = new
                {
                    message = "Hello from C# client with certificate!",
                    timestamp = DateTime.UtcNow
                };
                string jsonPayload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(payload);
                HttpContent content = new StringContent(jsonPayload, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

                Console.WriteLine("\nSending POST request...");
                Console.WriteLine($"Payload: {jsonPayload}");

                // 5. Send the POST request
                HttpResponseMessage response = await client.PostAsync(targetUrl, content);

                // 6. Process the response
                Console.WriteLine($"\nResponse Status Code: {(int)response.StatusCode} ({response.StatusCode})");
                string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();

                if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
                {
                    Console.ForegroundColor = ConsoleColor.Green;
                    Console.WriteLine("Request successful!");
                    Console.ResetColor();
                    Console.WriteLine("Response Body:");
                    Console.WriteLine(responseBody);
                }
                else
                {
                    Console.ForegroundColor = ConsoleColor.Yellow;
                    Console.WriteLine("Request failed or returned non-success status.");
                    Console.ResetColor();
                    Console.WriteLine("Response Body (if any):");
                    Console.WriteLine(responseBody);
                }
                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode(); // Optionally, throw an exception for non-2xx status codes
            }
        }
        catch (HttpRequestException e)
        {
            Console.ForegroundColor = ConsoleColor.Red;
            Console.WriteLine($"\nHttpRequestException: {e.Message}");
            if (e.InnerException != null)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Inner Exception: {e.InnerException.Message}");
            }
            Console.ResetColor();
            Console.WriteLine("Common causes for HttpRequestException with client certs:");
            Console.WriteLine("- Server not configured to accept/require client certs.");
            Console.WriteLine("- Client certificate not trusted by the server (e.g., CA mismatch).");
            Console.WriteLine("- Incorrect PFX password or corrupted PFX file.");
            Console.WriteLine("- TLS/SSL handshake failure (cipher suite mismatch, protocol version issues).");
            Console.WriteLine("- Network connectivity issues or firewall blocking the request.");
        }
        catch (System.Security.Cryptography.CryptographicException e)
        {
            Console.ForegroundColor = ConsoleColor.Red;
            Console.WriteLine($"\nCryptographicException: {e.Message}");
            Console.ResetColor();
            Console.WriteLine("This usually indicates an issue with loading the PFX certificate:");
            Console.WriteLine("- Incorrect PFX password.");
            Console.WriteLine("- Corrupted PFX file.");
            Console.WriteLine("- Insufficient permissions to access the private key (try running as admin or check key storage flags).");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.ForegroundColor = ConsoleColor.Red;
            Console.WriteLine($"\nAn unexpected error occurred: {ex.Message}");
            Console.ResetColor();
            Console.WriteLine(ex.StackTrace);
        }
        finally
        {
            Console.WriteLine("\nProgram finished.");
        }
    }
}
